ause coremltools has no py3.14 wheel)" :m4_ane_peak_tflops_vendor 38 :ane_utilization_at_4096 0.108 :shape_16_256_128 {:cpu_only_tflops 0.03 :cpu_and_gpu_tflops 0.03 :cpu_and_ne_tflops 0.033 :all_tflops 0.023} :shape_64_1024_512 {:cpu_only_tflops 1.021 :cpu_and_gpu_tflops 0.1 :cpu_and_ne_tflops 0.403 :all_tflops 0.378} :shape_128_2048_2048 {:cpu_only_tflops 2.639 :cpu_and_gpu_tflops 0.694 :cpu_and_ne_tflops 2.872 :all_tflops 2.85} :shape_256_4096_4096 {:cpu_only_tflops 2.614 :cpu_and_gpu_tflops 1.613 :cpu_and_ne_tflops 4.091 :all_tflops 4.133} :findings {:ane_is_silver_medal "ANE is the fastest dense fp16 path on M4 silicon (4.13 TFLOPS @ 4096^3 = 11% of vendor-claimed 38 TFLOPS peak), but the bit-packed AND/XNOR-popcount kernels beat it by 50-60% (6.31-6.82 TOPS). ANE wins on dense paths because no user-programmable XNOR exists; the only way to exceed ANE on M4 is custom Metal popcount kernels." :ane_not_programmable "Apple Neural Engine is closed silicon — no custom kernels, no XNOR-popcount path. Bench measures dense fp16 only. The 50-60% margin between bit-packed Metal and ANE is the silicon-level advantage of 1-bit on M4." :coreml_gpu_overhead "Core ML CPU_AND_GPU (1.61 TFLOPS) is HALF MLX direct (3.23 TFLOPS) at 4096^3. Core ML Metal dispatch has per-op overhead that compounds for single-op models. Use Core ML only for ANE-eligible models or whole-network compile; for arbitrary GEMM prefer MLX direct." :compiler_dispatch_choice "ALL ≈ CPU_AND_NE at 4096^3 (4.13 vs 4.09 TFLOPS), confirming the Core ML compiler chose to dispatch the matmul to ANE for this shape. For smaller shapes (64,1024,512) ALL drops to 0.378 TFLOPS vs CPU_ONLY 1.021 — small GEMMs are better off on CPU than ANE due to ANE setup cost."}} :evo_radeon_gfx1151 {:device "AMD Radeon(TM) 8060S Graphics" :arch "gfx1151" :total_mem_gb 60.0 :torch_version "2.9.1+rocm7.2.1" :hip "7.2.53211-158bd99533" :host "EVO-X2 NucBox (Windows + ComfyUI portable py3.12)" :shape_256_4096_4096 {:fp32_dense_tflops 0.89 :bf16_dense_tflops 9.3 :fp16_dense_tflops 9.54 :xnor_pytorch_swar_tops 0.088} :vs_mac_m4_4096 {:dense_bf16_ratio 2.88 :dense_fp16_ratio 2.92 :xnor_pytorch_ratio 2.44 :note "EVO Radeon 8060S has ~2.9x higher raw bf16/fp16 throughput than Apple M4 GPU at 4096^3. RDNA 3.5 matrix accelerators outperform M4's GPU matmul. Pure-PyTorch XNOR tracks the same ratio (both backends pay equal eager dispatch overhead)."} :blockers {:custom_hip_kernel "BLOCKED — Windows ComfyUI portable has no MSVC cl.exe AND no CUDA_HOME. torch.utils.cpp_extension.load() requires both even for hipcc-only HIP builds. Unblock via Visual Studio Build Tools 1.5 GB install + CUDA_HOME=any path, OR switch EVO to WSL2 + Linux torch ROCm." :triton_kernel "BLOCKED — no Triton Windows wheel on PyPI. Unblock via WSL2 + Linux triton." :quanto_int8_int4_int2 "CRASHED — optimum.quanto + Windows ROCm 7.2 has freeze() bug: 'linear() argument weight must be Tensor not NoneType'. Workaround: upgrade quanto or use quanto bf16-fallback path." :small_shape_timing "ROCm + Windows torch.cuda.synchronize doesn't flush for sub-microsecond ops; small shapes (16x256x128, 64x1024x512) report measurement-noise TFLOPS. Use 4096^3 numbers only."} :extrapolated {:expected_hip_xnor_tops_4096 18.0 :note "Extrapolation only — not measured. If the HIP build worked, the XNOR kernel should land at the same ~1.95-2.0x multiple of dense bf16 that the Metal kernel hit on Mac. Unblock HIP build to verify."} :ane_comparison_asymmetry {:m4_has_ane true :m4_ane_dense_fp16_tflops_4096 4.13 :evo_has_dedicated_nn_accelerator false :evo_equivalent_path "ROCm dense bf16/fp16 via matrix accelerators in RDNA 3.5 CUs" :evo_equivalent_tflops_4096 9.3 :note "AMD consumer Radeon (RDNA 3.5) does not ship a separate NN accelerator analogous to Apple ANE. The CU matrix units provide ~2.25x ANE throughput (9.30 vs 4.13 TFLOPS), but as a general-purpose GPU path rather than dedicated silicon. No 'ANE equivalent' to compare against directly."}} :key_findi